        "DEFINITION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "canonical_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Glomerulonephritis (GN) is a term often used to describe proteinuria of glomerular origin; however, GN is immune-mediated inflammation within the glomerulus (immune complex glomerulonephritis [ICGN]). Because this can only be diagnosed from a renal biopsy, many noninflammatory conditions (glomerulosclerosis, amyloidosis, etc.) may be incorrectly labeled GN in patients who have not undergone evaluation via renal histology.",
              "pdf_page": 648,
              "printed_page": 566,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Circulating antigen–antibody complexes become entrapped or antibodies can attach in situ to glomerular antigens or soluble antigens entrapped within the glomerular basement membrane; complement component C3 and/or immunoglobulins (Ig) G, M, or A may be involved in complexes, which alter glomerular permeability and induce inflammation, causing proteinuria, nephron injury, fibrosis, and progressive loss of renal function.",
              "pdf_page": 648,
              "printed_page": 566,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "ICGN has several different morphologies depending on the location of immunecomplex deposition as well as the glomerular response to injury; membranoproliferative, membranous nephropathy, proliferative, IgA nephropathy, and minimal change disease are recognized ICGN in small animals.",
              "pdf_page": 648,
              "printed_page": 566,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Inflammation and altered glomerular permeability result in proteinuria; subsequent oxidative damage and tubular obstruction cause renal injury, progressive nephron loss, and decreased glomerular filtration; azotemia and uremia may ensue.",
              "pdf_page": 648,
              "printed_page": 566,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hypercoagulability, hypoalbuminemia, and hypertension (HTN) are common complications.",
              "pdf_page": 648,
              "printed_page": 566,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SYSTEMS AFFECTED": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "canonical_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Renal/urologic—proteinuria is the initial symptom; both acute kidney injury (AKI) and chronic kidney disease (CKD) may occur secondarily.",
              "pdf_page": 648,
              "printed_page": 566,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cardiovascular—HTN is common; ascites and edema occur in nephrotic syndrome (NS); hypercoagulability and thromboembolism (TE).",
              "pdf_page": 648,
              "printed_page": 566,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Endocrine/metabolic—hypercholesterolemia in NS.",
              "pdf_page": 648,
              "printed_page": 566,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "GENETICS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "GENETICS",
          "canonical_heading": "GENETICS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Many breeds have inheritable glomerular disease leading to protein-losing nephropathy (PLN). Most are non-ICGN; however, several breeds are more frequently affected by GN (see below).",
              "pdf_page": 648,
              "printed_page": 566,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "INCIDENCE/PREVALENCE": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "INCIDENCE/PREVALENCE",
          "canonical_heading": "INCIDENCE/PREVALENCE",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Glomerular disease is present in most patients with kidney disease; however, not all are ICGN. Approximately 50% of dogs and cats",
              "pdf_page": 648,
              "printed_page": 566,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
